{
  "summary": "This computer has several ways for other machines to reach it and take control remotely, and the firewall that would normally shield them is off on the Public profile (the setting Windows uses on networks like cafés or airports). Two remote-control services are listening: TightVNC on port 5900 and Remote Desktop on port 3389, and Remote Desktop doesn't require the stronger sign-in check (Network Level Authentication). First, decide whether you actually use TightVNC and Remote Desktop, and turn the Public firewall back on. Also confirm you recognize the extra administrator account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "TightVNC remote control is installed and open to the network while the Public firewall is off",
      "finding_ids": [
        "FW-PROFILE-OFF:d0633f5d",
        "PORT-REMOTE-SERVICE:7cb92661",
        "REMOTE-ACCESS-TOOL:cb90e00a"
      ],
      "explanation": "TightVNC (C:\\Program Files\\TightVNC\\tvnserver.exe) is running and accepting connections on port 5900 from any network address. TightVNC lets someone see and control this computer from elsewhere. With the firewall off on the Public profile, nothing blocks that port when you join a public network. If you or someone you trust installed TightVNC on purpose, it may be expected, though it still shouldn't be exposed on public networks. If you don't recognize it, treat it seriously, because tools like this can be how someone gets in. The firewall setting also matters beyond TightVNC: it leaves every listening program reachable. The report shows the fix for each of these findings."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Remote Desktop is on, listening, and missing the stronger sign-in check",
      "finding_ids": [
        "RDP-NLA-OFF:8e943d20",
        "RDP-ENABLED:62fe1d90",
        "PORT-REMOTE-SERVICE:b128d5fc"
      ],
      "explanation": "Remote Desktop is turned on and listening on port 3389 for connections from other computers. It is one of the most attacked services on Windows. Network Level Authentication (NLA) is not required, so anyone who can reach the port gets to the Windows login screen before proving who they are. Together with the firewall being off on Public networks, this is a real exposure. If you don't use Remote Desktop, the report's fix for turning it off covers the whole group. If you do use it, the report shows how to require NLA."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "An extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The account <computer>\\<account-2> has full administrator rights. Having a separate admin account is often good practice, so this may be completely normal. Just confirm it's an account you or someone you trust created. An admin account you don't recognize would be a warning sign, especially on a computer with remote-access tools open. The report shows what to do if it isn't yours."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "REMOTE-ACCESS-TOOL:cb90e00a",
      "suggested_severity": "high",
      "reason": "TightVNC is actively listening on all network addresses while the Public firewall is off, so it is exposed in practice. It should get the same attention as the related high findings, unless the owner confirms they installed it."
    }
  ]
}
